.TH SHELF 1 "2026-08-06" "shelf 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
shelf \- every repo you have, on GitHub and on this machine
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shelf
[\fIcommand\fR] [\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B shelf
merges the repositories on your GitHub account with the git repositories on
this machine and reports them as one list. Each repo carries a state:
.B synced
(on GitHub and cloned here),
.B remote
(on GitHub, not cloned) or
.B local
(on this machine only, with no origin or an origin someone else owns). That
union is the point of the tool: neither
.BR gh (1)
nor the GitHub website can see unpushed commits, dirty worktrees, or a repo
that exists nowhere but this Mac.
.PP
GitHub data comes from a single GraphQL query and is cached; the local git
scan runs live on every invocation.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B triage
Default when no command is given. Every repo sorted by most recent activity,
with its push age, an eight-week commit sparkline, open issue count and local
state. Repos untouched for longer than \fBactiveDays\fR collapse into a single
\fIcold\fR line; \fB\-\-all\fR expands them.
.TP
.B audit
Five checks. \fIhygiene\fR: public repos missing a description, README, license
or topics (private repos are skipped and counted, since none of that is a
defect there). \fIstale\fR: no push in \fBactiveDays\fR and not archived.
\fIdead links\fR: \fBhomepageUrl\fR targets that did not answer 2xx. \fIheavy\fR:
larger than \fBheavyMb\fR, usually committed binaries. \fIunpublished\fR: local
repos with no remote. A check that finds nothing prints a tick rather than
being hidden.
.TP
.B show \fIrepo\fR
One repo on one screen: everything already cached, plus one live call for
recent commits and CI state. For a repo with no GitHub side the commits come
from the local log instead. The name is matched case-insensitively, and a near
miss suggests candidates.
.TP
.B index
Every public, non-fork repo grouped by language. This is the uncurated long
tail, meant to be written to a file with \fB\-o\fR and published.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-all
Include cold repos in triage instead of collapsing them.
.TP
.B \-\-refresh
Refetch from GitHub, ignoring the cache TTL.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine output: exactly one JSON value on stdout and nothing else. See
.B JSON OUTPUT
below.
.TP
.B \-\-html
Render in a browser. Starts a server on a random port bound to 127.0.0.1,
opens it, and runs until interrupted. The page sorts by any column and filters
across all rows, and its refresh button refetches live.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-out " " \fIFILE\fR
Write the HTML to \fIFILE\fR instead of serving it. The written page is static
and carries no refresh button.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-open
With \fB\-\-html\fR, do not launch the browser.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-links
With \fBaudit\fR, skip the homepage link check. Makes the command work with no
network.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show version and exit.
.SH SPARKLINE
The eight-week bar is scaled to that repo's own busiest week, so it shows the
shape of activity, not its magnitude; the adjacent commit count carries the
magnitude. Weeks run oldest to newest, left to right. A week with no commits is
the flat baseline, and any week with at least one commit rises above it, so
"quiet" and "nothing" are never the same glyph.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
.B shelf
reads
.B GITHUB_TOKEN
(or
.BR GH_TOKEN ),
and falls back to the token from
.BR "gh auth token" .
If neither is available it names both and exits non-zero. No token is ever
written to disk by shelf.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/shelf/config.json
Written with defaults on first run. Keys: \fBscanPaths\fR (roots to scan,
tilde allowed), \fBscanDepth\fR (how deep to walk, default 2), \fBactiveDays\fR
(the warm/cold threshold, default 90), \fBcacheTtlMinutes\fR (default 60),
\fBheavyMb\fR (default 20), \fBexclude\fR (repo names to drop entirely).
.TP
.I ~/.cache/shelf/repos.json
The cached GitHub response. Delete it to force a cold fetch;
.B \-\-refresh
does the same without deleting.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
Every read command accepts \fB\-\-json\fR. Errors stay human-readable on stderr
with a non-zero exit, so consumers branch on the exit code rather than parsing
error output.
.TP
.B shelf \-\-json
\fB{fetchedAt, cacheAgeMinutes, login, repos: []}\fR. Each repo is flat.
Every GitHub-side key (\fBvisibility\fR, \fBlanguage\fR, \fBcommits8w\fR,
\fBweeks\fR, \fBci\fR, ...) is \fBnull\fR when the repo is local-only, and
every local-side key (\fBpath\fR, \fBbranch\fR, \fBdirty\fR, \fBahead\fR,
\fBbehind\fR) is null when it is not cloned here. \fBnull\fR means it does not
apply, never that it could not be read; \fBdirty: 0\fR means a clean worktree
and \fBdirty: null\fR means there is no worktree.
.TP
.B shelf audit \-\-json
\fB{hygiene, stale, deadLinks, heavy, unpublished, privateSkipped,
linksChecked}\fR. The five checks are always arrays, empty when nothing was
found. \fBlinksChecked\fR is false when \fB\-\-no\-links\fR was given, so an
empty \fBdeadLinks\fR is never mistaken for a clean result.
.TP
.B shelf show \fIrepo\fR \fB\-\-json\fR
One repo object in the shape above, plus \fBrecentCommits\fR.
.TP
.B shelf index \-\-json
\fB{login, repos: []}\fR, public non-forks only.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success. 1 on an unknown command or option, a missing or unknown repo
name for \fBshow\fR, an unreadable config, or a GitHub failure with no cache to
fall back on. A refresh that fails while a cache exists prints a warning,
serves the cached data and exits 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B shelf
What is warm, what is cold, what is unpushed.
.TP
.B shelf \-\-all \-\-refresh
Everything, freshly fetched.
.TP
.B shelf audit \-\-no\-links
Every check that needs no network.
.TP
.B shelf index \-o repos.html
Write the public index to a file.
.TP
.B shelf \-\-json | jq '.repos[] | select(.state == "local")'
The repos that exist nowhere but this machine.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gh (1),
.BR git (1)
